      "body": "Off Leash update 25 November 2026\n\nCHINA\n•Shanghai’s Communist Party leader, Chen Jining, is being investigated on suspicions of “serious violations of discipline and the law” – which usually means allegations of corruption, in party parlance.\n•Chen is only the latest official probed in a widening crackdown on corruption and disloyalty, but his downfall particularly surprised analysts. He was a technocratic rising star in the party who was widely considered a candidate for the Politburo  Standing Committee, China’s top leadership body. He regularly lectured other officials on the importance of following Xi’s rules. As one expert explained: “If even Chen Jining has been taken down, the same could happen to others. There’s no safety for anyone.”\n•Separately, a U.S. federal jury found Chinese telecom giant Huawei guilty of 10 charges, including stealing trade secrets and skirting sanctions on Iran. The underlying allegations initially came to light in 2018 and resulted in the U.S. effectively banning the sale of Huawei products the following year. Huawei maintains its innocence and claims the charges were politically motivated.\n\nYEMEN\n•Yemen’s Houthis struck the Riyadh skyscraper housing Saudi Arabia’s sovereign wealth fund for the second time in a week, injuring 11. Worryingly, there was no evacuation warning this time, suggesting gaps in Saudi air defense.\n\nSUDAN\n•Ethiopia offered to mediate peace talks to end Sudan’s civil war, and Khartoum flatly rejected the offer, reiterating its accusation that Ethiopia “supports the rebellious paramilitary Rapid Support Forces” and that “drones had been launched from Ethiopian territory to target Sudan.” There’s decent evidence both claims are true.\n\nIRAQ\n•Iraq’s prime minister, Ali al-Zaidi, made another resource and power grab for the central government, wresting control of border crossings and oilfields in the semiautonomous region of Kurdistan.\n•Just days before, he imposed federal troops on areas controlled by the powerful Iran-backed Kataib Hezbollah militia after accusing it of smuggling oil.\n•Analysts consider Zaidi’s efforts to consolidate power with the federal government a gamble. If he pushes Kurds or militias too far, it could provoke an armed response that would leave Iraq even more fractured.",
